WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.233 --> 00:00:05.280
Ugh. What are they doing?!

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.220
You might use them to help clear a trench,

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.350
but the main weapon you'd
use would be grenades.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.642
I'm Alexander Watson.
I'm professor of history

00:00:12.642 --> 00:00:14.730
at Goldsmiths, University of London.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:16.290
I'm an expert on the First World War.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:17.520
I've written three books.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.800
And today we're going to
be looking at battle scenes

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.984
in First World War movies
to judge how real they are.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.910
The Germans have really made a lot

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.290
of effort on their trenches.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.980
They've brought their heavy
weaponry to defend the trenches,

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.710
but they've forgotten to
put any barbed wire down

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.120
at all in no-man's-land.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.335
You would've had loads and
loads of barbed wire on it.

00:00:44.335 --> 00:00:46.500
I mean, that's the key omission,
and it's a huge omission.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:47.741
Of course, they've done
that because they want her

00:00:47.741 --> 00:00:48.825
running towards them.

00:00:53.869 --> 00:00:57.660
By 1918, the German army wasn't
simply composed of riflemen.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.185
In 1918, if the German
army had been confronted

00:01:00.185 --> 00:01:03.150
by an invincible female superhero

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.100
and they were shooting at her with bullets

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.570
and that didn't work, then they can go

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:09.390
to a light machine gun, an MG 08/15.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.904
Or they've got a
minenwerfer, a light mortar,

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:15.690
7.58-centimeter mortar
that they can shoot at her.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.831
So, full credit to
"Wonder Woman" for that.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:26.755
These, kind of, shafts of
light that are being fired

00:01:26.755 --> 00:01:29.760
are tracer bullets, or incendiary bullets.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.860
And there were tracers used
in the First World War,

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:35.988
but they were mainly used
for anti-aircraft work,

00:01:35.988 --> 00:01:37.890
and by aircraft as well.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.220
They weren't used by
frontline machine guns.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:42.390
They would expose your position,

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:43.590
which you really didn't want to do,

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:46.705
because you'd bring down
artillery and mortar onto you.

00:01:46.705 --> 00:01:48.750
And second reason, it would
actually be a war crime.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.540
Using incendiary bullets
against enemy troops

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.751
was a war crime by the
standards of 1914 to '18.

00:01:55.751 --> 00:01:57.376
So it wasn't done.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:02.130
There's another error there.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.720
You've got a church in the background,

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.808
and that would be the first thing

00:02:05.808 --> 00:02:07.248
to be knocked down by heavy artillery,

00:02:07.248 --> 00:02:10.020
because it would be a
great observation post.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.620
You just didn't have high towers
directly behind the lines.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:14.995
Three out of 10.

00:02:14.995 --> 00:02:17.554
Come on, push forward!

00:02:19.707 --> 00:02:21.692
One thing that I do appreciate
about this scene is that

00:02:21.692 --> 00:02:24.697
you do actually have an officer
attempting to lead the men

00:02:24.697 --> 00:02:26.430
and keep some sort of control

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:28.710
and tell them where they're
going and direct them.

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:30.477
That's often forgotten.

00:02:30.477 --> 00:02:32.754
Gas! Gas!
[warning gong chiming]

00:02:32.754 --> 00:02:34.020
Gas!
[warning gong chiming]

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.186
OK, so this is all wrong.

00:02:35.186 --> 00:02:38.490
I mean, firstly, who brings a warning gong

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.560
into no-man's-land when you're on attack?

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.600
You know, they did use
warning gongs to signal

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.020
that there was a gas
attack, but in the trenches.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.510
Gas was used, famously,
in the First World War.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.793
It was used from 1915.
They started with chlorine.

00:02:50.793 --> 00:02:53.520
The Germans introduced
phosgene, which was more lethal.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.340
It's pretty light, slightly yellowish,

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.200
so it wasn't that easy to detect, either.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:02.335
But gas was an offensive
weapon, and that's because

00:03:02.335 --> 00:03:06.390
both chlorine and phosgene
are heavier than air.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.498
So if you're trying to take a trench,

00:03:09.498 --> 00:03:13.264
then if you plaster it
with phosgene or chlorine,

00:03:13.264 --> 00:03:16.687
that gas is going to
sink down into the trench

00:03:16.687 --> 00:03:19.470
and then down into the dugouts as well.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.570
Very, very dangerous for defending troops.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.945
Against attacking troops
in the open, though,

00:03:23.945 --> 00:03:25.620
you wouldn't use gas.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:27.630
These troops are
aboveground, not underneath,

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.070
but the gas sinks down.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:32.094
And your own defending
troops are, of course,

00:03:32.094 --> 00:03:33.660
below the ground in trenches and dugouts.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.350
So you're actually more
likely to risk them

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.150
rather than hurt the attacking troops.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:41.790
Phosgene, for example, it was lethal,

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:43.170
but it took a few hours

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:44.640
for soldiers to get really, really ill.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.370
If you're being attacked
by enemy soldiers,

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:49.650
really, you want an immediate impact,

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.108
before they get to your trench.

00:03:52.108 --> 00:03:53.820
So as a result, you use shrapnel.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:56.445
This isn't a plausible
scenario that we're watching.

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:03.120
The scene is implausible, but
we do get a really nice look

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.530
at a British PH helmet,
which was a mid-war gas mask.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.510
Unfortunately, he's wearing it wrongly.

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:13.290
You need an airtight seal
for this to work effectively.

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:15.281
And the way that they did that was

00:04:15.281 --> 00:04:19.202
they stuffed the bottom of
the mask down their collars.

00:04:19.202 --> 00:04:21.352
And he hasn't done that, so
he's going to be in trouble

00:04:21.352 --> 00:04:22.260
in a couple of hours' time.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:23.601
This is really a three,

00:04:23.601 --> 00:04:26.809
because they could at least
get the gas mask fitting right.

00:04:26.809 --> 00:04:27.750
You know?

00:04:34.011 --> 00:04:35.370
You can feel the power

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:39.090
of these 1918 Saint-Chamond French tanks.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:40.890
And on top of that, I
love how they have them

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:42.240
looming through the smoke.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.160
They're actually very
vulnerable to artillery fire.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:45.335
And so what the French did was

00:04:45.335 --> 00:04:47.730
they would put down a smoke
barrage, smoke shells,

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:49.650
in front of the tanks to obscure them,

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:52.050
and then they would come out
toward the enemy trenches.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:54.108
And that's really,
really well represented.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:04:59.760
So, there's problems as well

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.290
with how the Germans are behaving here.

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:03.180
The trench that they're defending

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.710
is actually a French trench,

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:07.056
and these French tanks
are counterattacking.

00:05:07.056 --> 00:05:08.660
And the thing about
trenches is they were built

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:10.110
to defend one way.

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:11.820
So you'd have the fire step

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:13.620
opposite the enemy, facing the enemy.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.050
You'd have the machine-gun emplacements

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:17.400
facing the enemy as well.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.175
But in this trench, miraculously,

00:05:20.175 --> 00:05:22.191
all of the fire steps, the defenses,

00:05:22.191 --> 00:05:24.535
suddenly seem to be facing,
if you like, the wrong way,

00:05:24.535 --> 00:05:27.150
towards the French counterattackers.

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:29.200
And it wouldn't have happened.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.458
In 1918, if you saw
tanks coming towards you,

00:05:31.458 --> 00:05:33.275
as a German soldier,
the first thing you do

00:05:33.275 --> 00:05:35.542
is you get your flare gun
and you shoot a flare,

00:05:35.542 --> 00:05:39.320
a signal flare, out over to
alert the artillery behind you

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:41.130
to bring down a covering bombardment.

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:42.510
That's your best chance of survival,

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:44.070
the best chance to hold a trench.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:45.780
And there's nothing of that here.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.450
By 1918, the Germans have
a number of nasty tricks

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:50.820
up their sleeves in order to fight tanks.

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.910
They've got light mortars,
so-called minenwerfer,

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:57.210
which can be turned horizontal
to fire directly at a tank.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:59.010
They've even got specialized
anti-tank weapons.

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:01.950
They've got anti-tank rifles,
great, big, huge rifles

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:05.580
which would give this
13-millimeter shot out,

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:07.950
which could penetrate a tank's armor.

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:09.900
But these guys are just being presented

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.270
as pretty helpless riflemen.

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:21.480
Why would you stop and shoot?

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:22.890
These French tanks, they've got

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.170
a 75-millimeter cannon on them.

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:28.560
They're intended to take out strongpoints

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.150
and to take out artillery

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:31.230
which could be threatening the tank.

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:32.588
There's absolutely no reason

00:06:32.588 --> 00:06:35.910
simply to shoot straight in
front of them at this trench.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:37.140
There's nothing really
to be gained by that.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:39.510
This is a 23-ton tank,

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:41.943
and it's got a 94-horsepower engine in it.

00:06:41.943 --> 00:06:44.850
That's a smaller engine than
your average modern estate car.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:47.700
And that's pulling this huge, huge weight.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:50.730
And if you stop, you might get stuck.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:52.443
The thing about war in 1918

00:06:52.443 --> 00:06:54.540
is that you keep moving all the time.

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:56.406
You keep moving, or you're killed.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:05.013
So, this tank appears to be
entering the trench network.

00:07:05.013 --> 00:07:07.470
If a tank is that far
down, it's very unlikely,

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:09.480
especially with these ones,
which were very front-heavy

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:11.700
because of that big 75-millimeter gun,

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.258
it's very unlikely to get out again.

00:07:20.836 --> 00:07:22.530
OK, now this is plausible.

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:24.870
If the tanks were separated
from their infantry,

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:27.900
then German defenders were
able to knock them out.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:28.890
Now, what these guys are doing is,

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:31.170
they haven't got any
weapon which can penetrate

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:32.610
the armor of the track tank.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:34.740
So they're going for what's
called a mobility kill.

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:38.010
They're blowing off the
tracks using a hand grenade,

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:40.779
which will stop it, and
then they're able to put

00:07:40.779 --> 00:07:44.430
a hand grenade into
one of the firing ports

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:47.220
and blow the crew to
smithereens inside it.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:50.490
That is how German infantry
combated tanks in 1918.

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:51.757
It was one of the methods.

00:07:56.159 --> 00:07:57.834
OK, stop. [sighs]

00:07:57.834 --> 00:07:59.910
Ugh. What are they doing?!

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:01.530
The French have come in.
They've got flamethrowers.

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:03.810
Flamethrowers were used. They
were used from 1915 onwards.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:04.830
The Germans introduced them.

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:07.380
The French had their own
mobile flamethrowers as well.

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:09.454
They didn't carry a huge amount
of liquid. That's one thing.

00:08:09.454 --> 00:08:10.980
And they weren't used in this way, either.

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:13.890
You might use them to help clear a trench,

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:16.140
but the main weapon you'd
use would be grenades.

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:18.048
But the bigger issue is
you wouldn't have them

00:08:18.048 --> 00:08:19.320
in the first wave like this.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:21.090
What these French guys are doing is

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:23.910
they are creating an
impenetrable wall of fire

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:26.601
through which no French troops can go.

00:08:26.601 --> 00:08:29.400
Meanwhile, the Germans,
who are retreating,

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:30.900
are going to get away.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:32.010
They're going to re-entrench,

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:34.068
and they're going to shoot everybody down.

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:42.480
The chaos and the fear,
there's a visceral sense here.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.460
I mean, I think that is pretty realistic.

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:48.510
But this scene couldn't have
happened in October 1918,

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:51.180
and that's actually misleading people

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.820
about the end of the First World War.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:55.500
The fighting that's being portrayed here

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:58.110
may well have happened
in the summer of 1918,

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:01.470
but for October 1918, it's gone.

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:04.380
The French and the British,
they roll 100 miles

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:05.880
in the last three months of the war.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.580
This is really, really mobile fighting,

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:10.440
but it's just not represented here.

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:13.274
So I'd give this, at
most, a five out of 10.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:23.220
So, here what we see is
Manfred von Richthofen,

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:24.743
the famous Red Baron,

00:09:24.743 --> 00:09:28.320
the No. 1 scoring fighter
ace of the First World War.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:30.060
He shot down 80 enemy airplanes.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:32.423
And he's leading his Jagdgeschwader 1,

00:09:32.423 --> 00:09:35.638
so his flight wing, fighter wing one.

00:09:35.638 --> 00:09:37.740
And he was known as the Red Baron

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:40.290
because he painted his aircraft red.

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:43.760
This is his, I think, his
final aircraft, a Fokker.

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:45.990
Fokker Triplane, the most
famous aircraft he flew.

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:47.867
They did paint their aircraft

00:09:47.867 --> 00:09:50.190
in these amazing bright colors.

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:52.290
The Germans had these wonderful reds

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:53.820
and yellows and patterns.

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:55.755
There wasn't any tactical reason for it.

00:09:55.755 --> 00:09:58.620
It wasn't really about
recognizing other pilots.

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:00.553
Some people say that Richthofen

00:10:00.553 --> 00:10:03.278
painted his aircraft
red to scare the enemy,

00:10:03.278 --> 00:10:07.432
but really these German pilots
did it because they could.

00:10:07.432 --> 00:10:09.206
They liked it. So they did it.

00:10:13.225 --> 00:10:16.290
It does give a sense of the sheer chaos.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:21.071
In 1918, a quarter of
the German air force,

00:10:21.071 --> 00:10:23.520
of German fighter pilots,
are shot down and killed.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.379
That's how lethal this type of combat was.

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:35.760
You would see scenes like
this in the Second World War,

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.817
particularly in and around,

00:10:38.817 --> 00:10:42.041
I guess above, big cities,

00:10:42.041 --> 00:10:44.326
with unmanned lighter-than-air balloons,

00:10:44.326 --> 00:10:46.230
which were called barrage balloons.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:50.688
And their purpose was
to force enemy bombers

00:10:50.688 --> 00:10:52.037
to fly higher,

00:10:52.037 --> 00:10:54.450
because otherwise, they'd clip their wings

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:55.723
and get knocked out of the air

00:10:55.723 --> 00:10:59.190
by the wires connecting
these balloons to the ground.

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:01.650
You do get barrage balloons
in the First World War,

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:03.390
but there's not so much use of them.

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:04.710
You wouldn't have them strung along

00:11:04.710 --> 00:11:06.060
the Western Front like this.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:08.190
So what you'd see on the Western Front

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:09.660
would be observation balloons.

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:12.201
The purpose was to

00:11:12.201 --> 00:11:15.701
see over no-man's-land into enemy lines.

00:11:15.701 --> 00:11:17.220
They had baskets underneath them.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:22.039
You'd have an observer with binoculars.

00:11:22.039 --> 00:11:25.050
And around the tether connecting
the balloon to the ground,

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:26.999
there would also be a telephone cable.

00:11:26.999 --> 00:11:28.657
And they were really, really important

00:11:28.657 --> 00:11:33.152
in making sure that fire, the
artillery fire, was accurate.

00:11:33.152 --> 00:11:36.180
So it was really, really
important to knock them down.

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:37.080
The thing is, is that

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:39.771
you'd only have two or three per sector.

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:47.603
Taking down these balloons

00:11:47.603 --> 00:11:49.320
sounds like it should be pretty easy,

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:52.140
because they're full of
highly flammable hydrogen.

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:53.400
But actually, it was really difficult,

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.020
and it was really, really dangerous.

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:57.540
If you fired a machine gun
into one of these balloons,

00:11:57.540 --> 00:11:59.190
it would pierce the balloon.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:00.720
But the material was pretty tough.

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:01.935
And what you would get is simply

00:12:01.935 --> 00:12:04.293
tiny machine-gun-bullet holes,

00:12:04.293 --> 00:12:06.717
which a little bit of
gas would seep out of,

00:12:06.717 --> 00:12:09.246
but not enough to bring the
balloon down in any way.

00:12:09.246 --> 00:12:11.370
And it was very, very dangerous to do,

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:14.070
because around these
balloons on the ground

00:12:14.070 --> 00:12:16.230
were anti-aircraft weapons,

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:19.200
and First World War planes are vulnerable.

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.540
They don't have self-sealing fuel tanks.

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:24.570
So if the fuel tanks get hit,
you've got petrol coming out.

00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:26.430
They're made of canvas and wood.

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:29.280
It doesn't take a huge
amount to bring them down.

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:31.260
With these anti-aircraft batteries

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:33.510
directly below the balloons,

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:35.190
most pilots would steer clear of them.

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:37.056
Very, very dangerous
job, taking them down.

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:45.810
What this pilot is doing is firing a flare

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:48.260
at the balloon, a signal flare,

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:50.335
which then lights the gas

00:12:50.335 --> 00:12:51.843
coming out of the
machine-gun-bullet holes.

00:12:51.843 --> 00:12:54.960
Now, I'm really doubtful that
that actually would've worked.

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:58.320
It's kind of a nice idea,
but flares are for signaling,

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:00.330
and I think most likely
what would've happened

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:02.259
is that if you fired a flare
at one of these balloons,

00:13:02.259 --> 00:13:03.665
it simply would've bounced off.

00:13:03.665 --> 00:13:04.839
It's a bit of a mix there,

00:13:04.839 --> 00:13:06.150
but I was pleased that at least they show

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:08.670
that these balloons
aren't easy to take down.

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:10.270
I'd give this a five.

00:13:15.737 --> 00:13:18.595
There are around 85,000 Indian troops

00:13:18.595 --> 00:13:21.390
who served on the Western Front
during the First World War.

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:22.500
And they often get ignored,

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:24.750
so it's nice to see that recognized here.

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:27.828
The British Indian army

00:13:27.828 --> 00:13:29.940
sent over an expeditionary
force to the Western Front.

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:31.483
So in order to increase their manpower,

00:13:31.483 --> 00:13:33.833
the British called over parts of

00:13:33.833 --> 00:13:35.708
the Indian forces under their command.

00:13:35.708 --> 00:13:38.760
Indian troops served on the Western Front,

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:40.320
actually, right through to 1918.

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:42.567
But the bulk of them in the infantry

00:13:42.567 --> 00:13:46.789
were fighting on the Western
Front in 1914 and 1915.

00:13:46.789 --> 00:13:51.602
And this shows an action
by the Lahore Division,

00:13:51.602 --> 00:13:54.517
which had Sikh troops in it, in 1915.

00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:01.050
Nice detail here, too.

00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:02.760
This is a subedar-major.

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:05.010
Every battalion of Indian troops had one.

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:08.524
He was the highest-ranking Indian soldier

00:14:08.524 --> 00:14:11.010
in an Indian battalion.

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:13.440
And these guys were very
well-respected soldiers.

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:15.390
They would've been long-service soldiers

00:14:15.390 --> 00:14:17.430
in their 40s or in their 50s.

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:20.100
And you can see him
leading the soldiers on,

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:21.459
just as would've happened.

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:28.470
You have this aerial attack

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:30.843
which appears to come from behind them,

00:14:30.843 --> 00:14:32.002
which in itself is odd,

00:14:32.002 --> 00:14:34.743
by aircraft which are dropping bombs.

00:14:34.743 --> 00:14:38.515
Now, there were ground-attack aircraft,

00:14:38.515 --> 00:14:40.748
but not this early, in 1915.

00:14:40.748 --> 00:14:45.748
There were aircraft which
dropped bombs behind enemy lines

00:14:45.748 --> 00:14:47.370
on things like rail stations

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:50.790
and targets of military significance.

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:54.360
But this idea that you
had biplanes swooping down

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:58.890
at very low level and
attacking ground troops,

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:01.078
it just didn't happen in 1915,

00:15:01.078 --> 00:15:04.530
not least because these aircraft
are very, very vulnerable.

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:06.005
They're very, very vulnerable to

00:15:06.005 --> 00:15:08.913
machine-gun fire, rifle fire.

00:15:08.913 --> 00:15:12.690
They're not moving that quickly,
and they're not armored.

00:15:12.690 --> 00:15:16.350
So airplanes kept pretty high up in 1915.

00:15:16.350 --> 00:15:17.983
This just simply wouldn't have happened.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.780
The Germans seem to be
firing British weaponry.

00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:26.970
There's a Lewis gun, a
British light machine gun,

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:28.675
that they're firing, and they,

00:15:28.675 --> 00:15:32.758
the Germans did use
looted Lewis machine guns,

00:15:32.758 --> 00:15:34.783
but not in 1915.

00:15:34.783 --> 00:15:36.570
The Lewis machine gun
still wasn't on the front

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:38.490
at the time this is going on.

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:40.770
The mortar, I think, is
probably Second World War,

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:42.014
post-Second World War.

00:15:42.014 --> 00:15:44.739
No one really knows. Even they
don't know what's happening.

00:15:49.241 --> 00:15:52.200
Oh, oh, and then the
trench as well is rubbish.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:53.220
You know, where's the parapet?

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:54.960
And where's the parados as well?

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:56.280
This is only going to
bother someone like me.

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:57.234
I appreciate that.

00:15:57.234 --> 00:16:01.230
But it's, nonetheless,
this is not a good trench.

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:03.120
It's great to see Indian
troops represented,

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:05.100
but they could have actually represented

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:06.150
what these men went through.

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:07.560
This is just fantasy.

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:09.010
This is a one.

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:16.941
This clip portrays

00:16:16.941 --> 00:16:20.266
the morning of the 25th of April, 1915,

00:16:20.266 --> 00:16:23.502
when Australian troops stormed ashore

00:16:23.502 --> 00:16:26.834
on what later became called
Anzac Beach at Gallipoli,

00:16:26.834 --> 00:16:30.600
fighting the Turks, against
a small band of defenders.

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:32.310
Not quite as small as shown in this film.

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:33.690
There were about 90 of them.

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:35.100
They've got the landscape right.

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:38.460
It was only a small beach
these Australians ended up on,

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:41.220
and it was surrounded by high hills

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:43.491
with steep slopes and cliffs.

00:16:45.379 --> 00:16:47.256
Attack!

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:50.850
The Turks only start firing

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:55.050
once the Australian
soldiers get off the boats.

00:16:55.050 --> 00:16:56.280
Why would you do that?

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:57.780
The Australians are in boats.

00:16:57.780 --> 00:17:00.930
They've got nowhere to go.
They haven't got any cover.

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:03.690
If they dive into the water,
they're carrying heavy packs,

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:05.040
they are going to drown,

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:06.930
and they can't do anything to you at all.

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:10.500
So if you're a defender and
you're facing an armada,

00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:13.770
you start shooting before
anyone gets out of the boat.

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:17.520
In reality, the Turks
started shooting, of course,

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:19.395
when the boats came in rifle range.

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:26.370
The other big mistake
are these machine guns

00:17:26.370 --> 00:17:27.990
in the bows of these landing craft.

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:29.580
You're going to hit your own people!

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:31.110
Clearly, you're going
to hit your own people.

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:32.100
Everyone is going to die,

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:33.758
and it's not going to be
the Turks who kill them!

00:17:38.790 --> 00:17:40.458
One thing about this scene as well

00:17:40.458 --> 00:17:44.610
is that it exaggerates the
bloodiness of the landing.

00:17:44.610 --> 00:17:46.740
There were about 50
casualties on either side.

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:49.980
It wasn't as bloody as
is being presented here.

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:51.980
So,

00:17:51.980 --> 00:17:53.505
a three.

00:17:53.505 --> 00:17:55.446
My favorite representation of the war

00:17:55.446 --> 00:17:56.550
would be "Journey's End."

00:17:56.550 --> 00:18:00.660
Not so much for any
realism of battle scenes.

00:18:00.660 --> 00:18:02.985
In some ways, that's
actually less important.

00:18:02.985 --> 00:18:05.051
But just for how

00:18:05.051 --> 00:18:08.918
it so realistically represents

00:18:08.918 --> 00:18:11.310
the tension and the claustrophobia

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:14.640
of waiting for an attack
in the First World War.

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:15.690
Thanks so much for watching.

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:17.760
If you want to see more
videos in this series,

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:18.960
click on the link above.

